From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 14:29:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: serve VK_KHR_pipeline_binary

The local protocol series gives the extension's five commands their command
ids and codecs, so the renderer can now dispatch them. Four are plain
forwards; vkCreatePipelineBinariesKHR creates objects and needs the renderer's
object tracking, because the guest names each VkPipelineBinaryKHR by an id it
chose and later destroys it by that id.

The command is unusual in two ways the dispatch has to respect. Its count is
in-out inside VkPipelineBinaryHandlesInfoKHR, and a driver asks how many
binaries a pipeline yields by passing no handle array at all, so a query
carries no object ids and must create nothing. And when the guest does offer
handles, the driver may fill fewer than it was given, so only the entries the
driver wrote become objects; the rest are released and returned as
VK_NULL_HANDLE. VK_INCOMPLETE is a success for this purpose: it means the
driver filled every handle it was offered and has more to give.

VkPipelineBinaryKHR joins the object handle union and the recognized object
types, and the device teardown path destroys any binary the guest left behind.

vkGetPipelineKeyKHR forwards a request with no create info, the key of the
device itself, and refuses a create info whose chain does not begin with a
graphics, compute or ray tracing pipeline's. The driver reads the first
structure of that chain as the create info of one of those pipelines without
checking its type, so a request that arrives with another structure first, or
with none, would have it read the fields of one structure as another's, past
the end of what the codec decoded. The refused request is answered with
VK_ERROR_VALIDATION_FAILED, and its reply carries a zeroed key: the codec
decodes only the type and chain of the key, so its size and bytes would
otherwise be whatever the decoder's temp pool held last.

vkGetPipelineBinaryDataKHR drops the blob from a reply whose data size grew
across the call. Every other command that returns a blob beside an in-out size
can only shrink it, so the codec measures the reply's blob by the size the
driver reports and reserves room for it from the size the guest offered. This
command breaks that: a buffer too small for the binary is answered with
VK_ERROR_NOT_ENOUGH_SPACE_KHR, the size the caller needs, and no data at all.
Encoding a blob of the grown size then writes past the reply region the guest
reserved, and the guest decodes a blob longer than its own reply stream, which
aborts the process. A reply that carries no blob is what the specification
describes and what the existing codec already reads, so the guest learns the
size it needs and its buffer is left alone.

vkCreatePipelineBinariesKHR refuses an import whose keys claim a size other
than a key's. The codec decodes keySize as a plain field beside a key array
that always holds VK_MAX_PIPELINE_BINARY_KEY_SIZE_KHR bytes, so the field says
nothing about how many key bytes arrived, and a driver that reads keySize
bytes reads past the decoded key. The driver refuses the same sizes itself;
the renderer does not rely on that, and refuses the whole call before
forwarding it, with VK_ERROR_VALIDATION_FAILED, so no object is made for any
of its binaries. The driver keeps the binaries it made before a failure and
returns the first failure it met; a result the renderer does not publish
destroys them, since no object names them. VK_PIPELINE_BINARY_MISSING_KHR is a
success code of the command and is published like VK_INCOMPLETE: the entries
the driver could not make are returned as VK_NULL_HANDLE, so the guest does not
keep an id for a binary that does not exist.

The commands that take a handle also refuse what would reach the driver as a
null pointer. The codec's lookup yields NULL for object id 0 and leaves the
refusal to the handler; an id the context never made, or made and destroyed,
is refused by the lookup itself. So vkGetPipelineBinaryDataKHR and
vkReleaseCapturedPipelineDataKHR end the context when the binary or the
pipeline they read is null, and so do vkCreateGraphicsPipelines,
vkCreateComputePipelines and vkCreateRayTracingPipelinesKHR when a
VkPipelineBinaryInfoKHR they chain names a null binary.
vkDestroyPipelineBinaryKHR still takes a null handle as the no-op the
command defines. vkCreatePipelineBinariesKHR answers
VK_ERROR_VALIDATION_FAILED to a request that names none of the keys and data
to import, a pipeline and a pipeline create info, because the driver would
read the import the request does not have.

